A||||4||Predator||Air to Air Intercooler||||5||
Z||000000||Predator||11-09-2000||05:51 PM||||I have read through the threads for information on intercoolers here. Good reading  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, but I have a question that I could not get answered through the research. I have a Turbo Kit I am installing that has a Air to Air Intercooler with it. I know water intercoolers are better, but that is not what I have. I know there is very limited space in the Fiero for a Air Intercooler specially with the heat from the engine compartment. My question is, has anybody done this, or can it even be done? I can't think of anywhere to mount it that will be where it can get freash air and out of the heat of the engie compartment without putting it up front which would be impractical. My Fiero is a 1985 GT manual. Any suggestions would be appreciated. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||BigTurbo87GT||11-09-2000||06:51 PM||||I am working on a turbo install as well. I'm thinking I can clear enough space where the current air intake is to fit a smallish intercooler. IF I get a large scoop of some kind, there are several kits that may suffice with a little modification. After moving the battery to the front I think there will be room on the right side to do the same. I believe there is quite a bit of empty space between body panel and inner fender/engine compartment on both sides. The inner fender could be louvered to aid flow.<BR>I haven't pulled the inner fender out yet to see how much space there is to work with yet though.<P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Clean stocker<BR>95 Z28, Clean not so stocker||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||11-10-2000||09:01 AM||||the stock air/liquid intercooler unit from a syclone/typhoon will fit where the fiero air canister is. you'll have to contact a local muffler shop for oval piping for the duct work instead of round though. if you have an air/air.. it's better than nothing just get a push/pull fan and strap it to the intercooler where ever you have room. where the stock air filter canister is would be great and just have the fan blowing up out of the engine compartment.. the car already breaths that way don't try to force air the other way. (down into the engine bay you just disrupt the air flow and cause more problems)  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula 18||11-10-2000||09:26 PM||||I have been thinking about this too and thought to either put it where the air canister is now but that's a lot of air forced into the engine bay or to put one behind where the black vents are on the driver's side with a small fan to push air out thought the trunk lid since this is how the engine bay is cooled anyway. <BR>I was thinking about using a Air to Air intercooler off a 87-88 ford thunderbird coupe and the adding louvered vents like it has. This location would leave little plumbing to do since this is where the compressor outlet is. <P>Another alternative is Water Injection. You can inject water or and mix of water an Methanol either before or after the compressor. If you do it after the Compressor it gets a little more tricky but it won't chew up the compressor wheel either. You can cool about 150 degrees for about $100 if you can build the kit yourself.  check out this link for some more info <A HREF="http://home.ccci.org/Key_Information/MerkurPage.htm#H20" TARGET=_blank>http://home.ccci.org/Key_Information/MerkurPage.htm#H20</A>  Injection<P>OK I am done taking up space now.<P>------------------<BR>Brian<BR> -88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wannabe||11-10-2000||10:48 PM||||Another intercooler that will fit is from the  Eagle Talon/Mitsubishi Eclipse. These are cheap, plentiful and compact. The Fiero Shop (Racetech) was doing an install using one of these. Any air to air intercooler that fits under the hood is going to be too small to really do a good job, but something is better than nothing. For more ideas, check out some of the info on the MR2 Turbo.||||reg||1
